WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.150
In week six of a programming course, a

2
00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:05.670
third of students say that after a

3
00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:09.370
equals b, changing b also changes a.

4
00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:12.130
They passed every coding quiz.

5
00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:13.470
How?

6
00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:17.490
Today: the hidden models students hold

7
00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:20.530
of how programs run, the research on

8
00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:22.930
novice misconceptions, and tracing

9
00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:24.230
activities that fix them.

10
00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:28.490
A notional machine is an idealised

11
00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:30.810
computer that explains how a language

12
00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:33.590
runs, at the level a learner needs.

13
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.180
Students always build some mental model.

14
00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:39.500
Our job is to make sure

15
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:40.680
it is a viable one.

16
00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.360
Sorva's review catalogues dozens

17
00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:45.700
of novice misconceptions.

18
00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.460
Assignment is seen as a lasting link.

19
00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:52.100
Variables are thought to hold

20
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:53.580
several values at once.

21
00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.400
And students assume the computer

22
00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.100
understands what they meant.

23
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.100
Here is a short trace.

24
00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.730
After three lines, a holds

25
00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:05.910
five and b holds seven.

26
00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:09.210
Students with the link model answer seven

27
00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:11.910
for both, and that wrong answer tells you

28
00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:13.630
exactly which model they hold.

29
00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:16.580
Research by Lister and

30
00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:18.500
colleagues suggests a sequence.

31
00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:21.290
Students who cannot trace code

32
00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:24.090
rarely explain it, and those who

33
00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:26.670
cannot explain it rarely write it.

34
00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:30.330
So assess tracing and explaining in

35
00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:32.190
plain English, not only writing.

36
00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:35.790
Draw memory as labelled boxes on the

37
00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:37.570
board for every lecture example.

38
00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.970
Use visualisers such as Python Tutor.

39
00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:43.930
And use multiple-choice tracing

40
00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:46.230
questions whose wrong options each

41
00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:47.610
match one known misconception.

42
00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:49.620
Quick check!

43
00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:54.580
b is five, then a equals b,

44
00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:56.760
then b becomes seven.

45
00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:00.190
What does print a, b show?

46
00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:02.660
Five and seven.

47
00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:04.920
Assignment copies the

48
00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:06.380
value at that moment.

49
00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.060
It does not create a lasting

50
00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:10.360
link between the two names.

51
00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:13.780
In the app, open the trace lab.

52
00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.760
Predict each variable after every

53
00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:19.560
line, then step through and compare

54
00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:21.040
your prediction with the machine.

55
00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:22.790
Your turn.

56
00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:26.320
A student answers seven, seven.

57
00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:28.530
Which misconception does

58
00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:29.430
that answer reveal?

59
00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:32.390
It reveals the belief that

60
00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:35.010
assignment creates a lasting link.

61
00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:38.700
Remember: every student holds a notional

62
00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.460
machine, tracing comes before writing,

63
00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.160
and wrong answers are diagnostic.

64
00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:48.510
Try it in your next lecture: start with

65
00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:50.670
three tracing questions, each with

66
00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.270
distractors that match a known

67
00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:52.610
misconception.

68
00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:55.060
Students vote on their phones.

69
00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:57.800
Then redraw memory for the

70
00:02:57.800 --> 00:02:59.560
question most students missed.
